---
import Files from "@/components/builders/Files.astro";
import Flow from "@/components/builders/Flow.astro";
import Pager from "@/components/builders/Pager.astro";
import RecordedNote from "@/components/builders/RecordedNote.astro";
import Shots from "@/components/builders/Shots.astro";
import Transcript from "@/components/builders/Transcript.astro";
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Docs from "@/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { recordedOf } from "@/lib/builders";
import { docHref } from "@/lib/docs";
import { LIVE_DEMOS, repoFile } from "@/lib/site";

const rec = recordedOf("astro");
const f = rec.facts;
const raw = import.meta.glob<string>(
  [
    "../../../../examples/platform-astro/src/**/*.{astro,tsx,ts}",
    "../../../../examples/platform-astro/{astro.config.mjs,wrangler.jsonc,package.json}",
  ],
  { query: "?raw", import: "default", eager: true },
);
const order = [
  "src/pages/index.astro",
  "src/components/Counter.tsx",
  "src/worker.ts",
  "src/pages/posts/[slug].astro",
  "src/pages/api/time.ts",
  "astro.config.mjs",
  "wrangler.jsonc",
  "package.json",
];
const files = Object.fromEntries(
  order.map((p) => [p, raw[`../../../../examples/platform-astro/${p}`] ?? ""]),
);
const buildSteps = rec.steps.filter((s) => s.request.url.includes(":8787"));
const runSteps = rec.steps.filter((s) => !s.request.url.includes(":8787"));
---

<Docs
  title="Astro builder"
  description="The platform's Astro builder: an Astro project built from its sources without Node or Vite, in a Durable Object, then bundled into one Worker that runs on Wrangler, celld and CellB. A working example with recorded results."
>
  <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-primary">Builders · 4 of 5</p>
  <h1>Astro builder: Astro sites built from their sources</h1>
  <p>
    Astro builds with Vite on Node. The platform's Astro builder does what Astro's build does for
    an on-demand (<code>output: "server"</code>) site without either: it runs the project's{" "}
    <code>astro.config.mjs</code> and its integrations, compiles the <code>.astro</code> files
    with Astro's compiler as WebAssembly, and bundles the server and the islands with esbuild. The
    bundler then turns the result into one Worker. It runs wherever esbuild runs: in a Durable
    Object, in a Worker Loader isolate, in a browser tab, or on Node.
  </p>

  <h2>What it is for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Sites built by a service.</strong> A CMS, a site builder or an agent sends sources
      and gets a deployable Worker back, with no build machine.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Astro in a browser tab.</strong> The same builder runs in the visitor's tab: try{" "}
      <a href={LIVE_DEMOS.astro.href}>{LIVE_DEMOS.astro.title}</a>, which builds an Astro app, or
      EmDash, in your browser and runs it there on CellB.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Real apps.</strong> EmDash, an Astro CMS with 225 routes, D1, KV, R2 and cron,
      builds from its sources in about 35 seconds on the Node runner, or 90 to 145 seconds inside
      the object on hosts without the 128 MB limit.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <Flow
    steps={[
      { label: "POST /builds", detail: "sources with an astro.config.*; no node_modules, no dist/", kind: "client" },
      { label: "astro.config + integrations", detail: "bundled and evaluated; hooks register routes, renderers, Vite plugins" },
      { label: "compiler-rs (WASM) + esbuild", detail: ".astro compiled; server and client bundles, as Astro's Vite build makes them" },
      { label: "bundler", detail: "server/entry.mjs into one worker.js, client/ into assets/" },
    ]}
    caption="The Astro builder writes what Astro's Cloudflare build writes (wrangler.json, server/entry.mjs, client/); the bundler takes it from there, so an Astro build gets the same host fixes as any Worker."
  />
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>The config runs first.</strong> <code>astro.config.mjs</code> is bundled, with Vite
      and Cloudflare tooling stubbed and Node modules over a virtual file system of the packages
      build-time code reads. Its integrations' <code>astro:config:setup</code> and{" "}
      <code>astro:config:done</code> hooks then run: here <code>@astrojs/react</code> registers
      its renderer.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Where that code runs depends on the host.</strong> The config is the project's code.
      workerd and celld refuse to evaluate code at run time, so there the whole build runs in a{" "}
      <a href={docHref("packages/cellb/docs/services/dynamic-workers.md")}>Worker Loader</a>{" "}
      isolate of its own. CellB and browsers evaluate the config directly.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Routes and pages.</strong> Routes come from <code>src/pages</code> and the
      integrations, ranked as Astro ranks them. Each <code>.astro</code> file is compiled by
      compiler-rs (its wasm32-wasi build; a browser tab must be cross-origin isolated for its
      shared memory).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bundles as Astro makes them.</strong> The server bundle is one module with Astro's{" "}
      <code>App</code>, the routes and every page. The client bundles hold the islands and the
      renderer's client entry, with production React and one React for the server and the islands.
      The scripts of the built-in client directives (<code>client:load</code>, <code>idle</code>,
      …) come from the project's Astro: in <code>astro</code> itself up to 7.4.4, in its
      <code>@astrojs/runtime</code> from 7.4.9.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Usage</h2>
  <p>
    The example is <a href={docHref("examples/platform-astro/README.md")}>a small Astro app</a>{" "}
    from the repository's examples: a home page that counts visits in a Durable Object, a React
    island, a dynamic route and an API route. Only its sources are sent: no{" "}
    <code>node_modules</code>, no <code>dist/</code>.
  </p>
  <Files
    files={files}
    source={{ label: "examples/platform-astro/", href: repoFile("examples/platform-astro/src/pages/index.astro") }}
  />
  <p>From a shell, in the app's folder:</p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="sh"
    code={`zip -r - . -x 'node_modules/*' 'dist/*' \\
  | curl -X POST --data-binary @- 'http://localhost:8787/builds?sourcemap=0'   # → { id, status, log, stats }
curl -o build.zip http://localhost:8787/b/<id>/project.zip && unzip build.zip -d build
npx wrangler dev -c build/wrangler.json`}
  />
  <p>
    The request is the bundler's: a project with an <code>astro.config.*</code> is built by the
    Astro builder first (<code>?kind=astro</code> forces it). Packages that integrations read from
    disk at build time go in <code>spec.astro.preload</code>; EmDash's are{" "}
    <code>{`[["emdash"], ["@emdash-cms/admin", "emdash"]]`}</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Results</h2>
  <RecordedNote recorded={rec} />
  <h3>The build</h3>
  <p>
    {(Number(f.buildMs) / 1000).toFixed(1)} s from the request to the answer, in the
    <code>BuildJob</code> object, with Astro and React downloaded from npm on the way. The log
    shows each phase.
  </p>
  <Transcript steps={buildSteps} />
  <h3>The output, running</h3>
  <p>The recorder unpacked <code>project.zip</code> and started it on CellB in Node:</p>
  <Shots shots={rec.shots} />
  <Transcript steps={runSteps} start={buildSteps.length + 1} />

  <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      On-demand rendering only: pages are not prerendered. Integrations' <code>injectScript</code>{" "}
      and client directives are ignored, and image services are replaced by the no-op one.
    </li>
    <li>
      The project's config runs with the build. On CellB and in browsers it runs in the bundler's
      object, with that object's bindings in reach: build only projects you trust there.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    API reference: <a href={docHref("packages/astro-builder/README.md")}>Astro builder</a>. The
    builder started as the one in <a href={docHref("examples/astro-in-browser/README.md")}>Astro
    built in a tab</a>.
  </p>
  <Pager slug="astro" />
</Docs>
